.TH MOUNT 1 "November 2026" "LikeOS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mount \- mount file systems, or list the mounted ones
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR types ]
.br
.B mount \-a
.RB [ \-fnv ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR types ]
.RB [ \-O
.IR options ]
.br
.B mount
.RB [ \-fnrvw ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR type ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR options ]
.I source directory
.br
.B mount
.RB [ \-fnrvw ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR options ]
.IR source | directory
.br
.B mount \-o remount\fR[\fB,\fIoptions\fR]
.I directory
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mount
attaches the file system on a device (or one that needs no device, such as
.BR tmpfs )
to a directory, the mount point, so that its files appear there.
.BR umount (1)
detaches it again.
.PP
Without arguments
.B mount
lists the mounted file systems, one line each,
.IP
.I source
.B on
.I directory
.B type
.I type
.BI ( options )
.PP
from
.BR /proc/mounts ;
.B \-t
limits the list to some types.
.PP
With one argument, a mount point or a source, the line of
.B /etc/fstab
for it is mounted, with that line's type and options (options given with
.B \-o
are added to them).
.B mount \-a
mounts every line of
.B /etc/fstab
that is not marked
.BR noauto ,
is not swap and is not mounted already, in the order of the file \(em what
.BR init (8)
does at start-up.
.PP
Mounting needs root; listing does not.
.SS Sources
.TP
.BI /dev/ device
A block device:
.BR /dev/sda1 ,
.BR /dev/nvme0n1p2 ,
.BR /dev/mmcblk0p1 .
.TP
.BI UUID= uuid
The device whose file system has this UUID
.RB ( blkid (8)
shows it).
.TP
.BI LABEL= label
The device whose file system has this label.
.TP
.BI PARTUUID= uuid
The partition with this unique partition GUID (GPT) or MBR identifier.
.TP
.BI PARTLABEL= name
The partition with this name (GPT).
.TP
.I word
A file system without a device:
.BR tmpfs .
.PP
The tags are looked up in
.BR /dev/disk/by\-uuid ,
.BR by\-label ,
.B by\-partuuid
and
.BR by\-partlabel .
Bind mounts and loop devices (a directory or a file as the source) are not
offered.
.SS File system types
.TP
.B ext4
The LikeOS root file system and data disks (also ext2 and ext3 file
systems, read and written as ext4).
.TP
.B vfat
FAT12, FAT16 and FAT32: the EFI system partition, USB sticks and memory
cards
.RB ( fat ,
.B fat32
and
.B msdos
are taken for the same).
.TP
.B tmpfs
A file system in memory, empty when mounted, gone when unmounted.
.PP
Without
.B \-t
the type is the one
.B /etc/fstab
gives, or else the kernel recognises the file system on the device.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Mount every file system of
.B /etc/fstab
that is not
.BR noauto .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-types " \fIlist\fR"
The file system type.
With
.B \-a
or a listing: the types to include, separated by commas; a
.B no
prefix excludes a type
.RB ( notmpfs ),
and a list starting with
.B no
excludes all of its types.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-test\-opts " \fIlist\fR"
With
.BR \-a :
only the lines that have these options
.RB ( no
.I option
selects the lines without it).
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-options " \fIlist\fR"
Mount options, separated by commas (below).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-read\-only
Mount read-only, the same as
.BR "\-o ro" .
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-rw ", " \-\-read\-write
Mount read-write, the same as
.BR "\-o rw" .
A device that is write-protected is mounted read-only when neither
.B rw
nor
.B \-w
was asked for.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fake
Do everything but the mount itself (with
.BR \-v :
show what would be mounted).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-mtab
Accepted; there is no
.B /etc/mtab
to update.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Say what is mounted, with which options.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-show\-labels
Accepted with a listing.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH MOUNT OPTIONS
The options every file system takes:
.TP
.BR ro ", " rw
Read-only, or read-write (the default).
.TP
.B defaults
.BR rw ,
.BR suid ,
.BR dev ,
.BR exec ,
.BR auto ,
.BR async .
.TP
.BR nosuid ", " suid
Ignore (or honour) the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits of programs.
.TP
.BR nodev ", " dev
Do not (or do) give access to device files on the file system.
.TP
.BR noexec ", " exec
Do not (or do) allow programs on the file system to be run.
.TP
.BR noatime ", " atime ", " relatime ", " strictatime ", " nodiratime ", " diratime
When the access times of files (and directories) are updated.
.TP
.BR sync ", " async ", " dirsync
Write data (or directory changes) at once, or when convenient.
.TP
.B lazytime
Keep time stamp updates in memory longer.
.TP
.B nosymfollow
Do not follow symbolic links on the file system.
.TP
.B remount
Change the options of a file system that is mounted already: its flags
.RB ( ro
and
.B rw
among them) are those it has, changed by the options given.
.TP
.BR noauto ", " auto
In
.BR /etc/fstab :
not mounted (or mounted) by
.B mount \-a
and at start-up.
.TP
.B nofail
In
.BR /etc/fstab :
a device that is not there is no error.
.TP
.BR X\-mount.mkdir [ =\fImode\fR ]
Make the mount point when it does not exist (with
.IR mode ,
octal; default 0755).
.PP
Anything else is the file system's own option and passed on to it.
.B ext4
has none.
.B vfat
takes
.BI uid= n
and
.BI gid= n
(the owner every file shows; FAT stores none),
.BI umask= octal\fR,
.BI fmask= octal
and
.BI dmask= octal
(the permission bits taken away from every file, from files, from
directories; the defaults give files 0644 and directories 0755), and
.BR shortname= lower | win95 | winnt | mixed
(how names that fit 8.3 are shown and created).
.B tmpfs
takes
.BI size= n
(bytes, with a
.BR k ,
.B m
or
.B g
suffix, or a percentage of the memory with
.BR % ;
default: a quarter of the memory),
.BI nr_inodes= n
(the most files),
.BI mode= octal
(of the root directory; default 1777),
.BI uid= n
and
.BI gid= n
(the owner of the root directory).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong usage, or no permission.
.TP
.B 2
A system error.
.TP
.B 32
The mount failed.
.TP
.B 64
Some of several mounts failed
.RB ( \-a ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/fstab
The file systems to mount, see
.BR fstab (5).
.TP
.B /proc/mounts
What is mounted now.
.TP
.B /dev/disk/by\-uuid\fR, \fBby\-label\fR, \fBby\-partuuid\fR, \fBby\-partlabel
Where the tags are looked up.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt
mount \-t vfat \-o uid=1000,gid=1000 LABEL=STICK /media/stick
mount \-t tmpfs \-o size=512m,mode=1777 tmpfs /scratch
mount \-o remount,ro /
mount \-a \-t notmpfs
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR umount (1),
.BR fstab (5),
.BR blkid (8),
.BR lsblk (8),
.BR findfs (8)
